    backlog:hasAuditNote "Build the page of chapter 5 again as a NEW file with a NEW page version (higher than the page 9.80.0 (not delivered as ready: one failed feature)) on the template 9.52.1 (course_page_template_v9_52_1.html, built by TK_Repair_Template; pass it with PAGE_TEMPLATE) with the page builder v4_15_0 and the page-data builder v2_24_0, from the same inputs as the earlier page (the deck's lecture export and stories, the chapter-code manifest chapter_code_ch05_v1_1_0.json, the pywheels file). Everything of the earlier build stays valid; the change is the template. Run the page tests 9.36.0, 9.34.1 and 9.35.1 (the runner with PAGE_TEST), serially, store the result as a new test_results file, and build the Stage 4 page ABox with the Stage 4 builder. MEASURED FACTS. The executor SEN0401-I17-page-ch05 reported the chapter 5 page 9.80.0 (commits c4002e1 to 0fb29ca, template 9.52.0, builders v4_15_0 and v2_24_0): the page tests 9.36.0, 9.35.1 and 9.34.1 each fail exactly one feature, 'a story chip on a concept opens that story in the Stories tab' (detail: story GoxColdFind, which lands at 2064 px, outside the 768 px viewport). Its diagnosis (confirmed by the builder session by reading template 9.52.0): the click handler of the story chips (the listener on the panes, button[data-story]) calls showTab('stories') and then scrollIntoView on the story at once; the pane height is fitted afterwards, so the scroll position is lost. The chapter 6 page 9.81.0 (executor SEN0401-I17-page-ch06, commits faf1b10 to ca2e510) passes all three tests, but it carries the same handler: the page tests click the first chip of the first concept that has one, and in chapter 6 that chip is the first story, which sits at the top of the pane and needs no scroll. So the defect is in template 9.52.0 and is latent in the chapter 6 page; the builder session re-ran the page tests on both pages on 2026-10-23 (results in the control version). PAGE STANDARD (checked by the new page test 9.36.0, Playwright 1.56.0): P1 no horizontal overflow at 390 px and at 1366 px; P2 the chapter-code tab lists the deck's examples by NAME in the deck's slide order, each runnable in the page with the chapter library defined in the page and its result visualised, and the code lives in the editor, not in the list; P3 tabs and left tree are synchronised and their label sets are disjoint or the tab bar is one row; P4 no third level of tabs, and one more level of panels so that one panel holds one concept; P5 a panel is at most 1.0 screen high at 1366x768; P6 no list repeated on one screen; P7 every diagram has zoom; P8 the top-level menu is uncrowded (at most 8 top-level tabs); P9 a text-size control and an adaptive layout at 390, 768 and 1366 px; P10 executed code results are visualised; P11 the page declares the standard and the examples equal the deck's code manifest. DOM CONTRACT shared by the template, the page test and the page tasks (so the independent test can find what the template builds): the chapter-code tab is the tab with attribute data-pane=\"chaptercode\"; its list items have data-cc-item, the NAME of the example in data-cc-name and the deck's order number in data-cc-order; an item opens the example in the editor element data-cc-editor with a run button data-cc-run and a result element data-cc-result; the left tree items have role=treeitem and the tabs role=tab; one concept panel is an element with data-panel; the playground list items have data-pg-item and show names only; a page that follows the standard declares <meta name=\"page-standard\" content=\"9.52\">. A committed file is never edited: every output is a new file with a new version number. STOP and report if the task cannot be satisfied without editing a committed file, a gate, a test or a threshold beyond the named new files. Report every result as printed (PASS, FAIL, NOT_MEASURED, CANNOT_RUN); a skip is not a pass. The machine has 2 cores: browser tests run one at a time and no build runs beside them. Planned session SEN0401-I18-page-ch05; estimated start T0+2.2h after the executors begin, about 2.0 hours (an estimate, G120: nobody has witnessed a start; the real start is recorded at work begun)." .

