ex:AC7_Deck_Ch08 backlog:hasAuditNote "Control 2026-10-24 (record v0.241.0), every element run by the evidence bridge and recorded as printed: (1) deck standard check v1_1_0, D1 to D12, exit 0: MET [deck standard check v1_1_0: D1 to D12 all PASS over the 45 de-duplicated key con: verified -> sen0401_deck_standard_check v1.1.0 | python-pptx 1.0.2 | chapter 08 | deck 03-materials/ch08/SEN0401_Ch08_DigitalSignatures_3e_v2_0_0.pptx FACTS slides=51 code_slides=20 native_charts=7 analogies=68 longest_box_words=27 (slide 21) D1 PASS every slide is number] | (2) Deck chapter check and deck match: MET [chapter check Deck/Ch08 v1_3_0: verified -> deck: 03-materials/ch08/SEN0401_Ch08_DigitalSignatures_3e_v2_0_0.pptx deck check: deck_check_v1_0_0.py 20 examples re-run on 51 slides; 0 mismatch(es) stale fixtures: ['fixture_stale_expr_v1_0_0.pptx'] refused: [True] ok fixture stale_pPr by sen0401_deck_struc; deck match check v1_0_0: 0 not carried (it compares the page 9.77.0 with the old: verified -> chapter 08 page 9_77_0: 17 items compared with lecture_v1_1_0.json and sen0401_ch08_stories_v1_2_0; not carried: 0; overlap scan v1_0_0: CLEAN (the executor's tool, not independent): verified -> RESULT CLEAN (0 findings)] | (3) manifest in slide order: MET [chapter-code manifest: order equals the order of the code slides, names unique, : verified -> manifest chapter_code_ch08_v1_0_0.json | 20 examples | 20 code slides | deck SEN0401_Ch08_DigitalSignatures_3e_v2_0_0.pptx | PASS order equals code-slide order, names unique, library files exist, hashes match where stated] | (4) no committed file edited: MET [no committed file edited (every file of the two commits added): verified -> nochange commits 488146a,adc96d3 | PASS every file of the commits was added, none modified or deleted]. RESULT: MET: all elements are met. Every check ran on the evidence bridge one after the other, on a machine with no other page test running. RENDERED REVIEW by the builder session (independent of the executor): the deck was rendered with soffice to PDF and PNG (51 pages, 800 x 450) and EVERY slide was viewed on its own, one image per slide, not in contact sheets. Seen: title, route, questions, concept map, seven dividers each with three or four concepts, a meaning line, an everyday comparison and the progress strip, concept-card slides with the everyday comparison under each meaning, twenty code slides each with the listing, a plain-language line under every step and the executed result as a table or a native chart, three story slides with 5N1K, picture, credit and live link, the policy/consensus slide, the BIP status chart, summary, resources and closing. Nothing overlaps, no text leaves its box, every slide is numbered. FINDINGS KEPT (none is an element of the criterion; the coordinator decides): (a) STORY PICTURES: the Schnorr slide (22) carries a real photograph of Claus-Peter Schnorr (Konrad Jacobs, Wikimedia Commons, credited); the signature-in-the-book slide (33) shows the Bitcoin logo and the Android slide (39) the Android robot, both real public pictures, credited and marked on the slide as stand-in pictures (no picture of the sender or of the incident was used); the deck standard check counts a real picture and passes D5, but the owner's story rule asks for real pictures of the story, so an event picture (for example a block explorer view of block 277,316 and the Bitcoin.org alert page) would serve it better. (b) The BIP status slide (48) is a native chart with counts taken from the saved BIP index of deck 1.1.0 and says so on the slide; it is not one of the twenty code examples, so its numbers are recorded, not run by this deck. (c) The WHO and WHERE chips of the three story slides are cut with an ellipsis (slides 22, 33, 39). (d) The route slide (2) lists eight entries under the words seven parts, and slide 4 counts seven parts. (e) Slides 8, 35 and several concept-card slides leave much empty space. (f) The closing slide lists four lines while slide 3 promises a return to seven questions. " .
