ex:TK_Ch08_Deck_Build backlog:hasAcceptanceCriterion ex:AC7_Deck_Ch08 ;
    backlog:hasEvidence ex:Ev36_std, ex:Ev36_overlap, ex:Ev36_deckchk, ex:Ev36_match, ex:Ev36_manifest, ex:Ev36_nochange, ex:Ev3C_File_c15119aee3, ex:Ev3C_File_ec9de881a8, ex:Ev3C_File_eee136077e, ex:Ev3C_File_7ce4c52b06, ex:Ev3C_File_8bef1c3cdf, ex:Ev3C_File_0468d05050, ex:Ev3C_File_5ad405dd52, ex:Ev3C_File_337084e53a, ex:Ev3C_File_cc4b64abe0, ex:Ev3C_File_fb416321f1, ex:Ev3C_File_bd2a4e7c88, ex:Ev3C_File_6e5ed79282, ex:Ev3C_File_bd7e82e94b ;
    backlog:hasAuditNote "Done, recorded 2026-10-24: delivered by the executor session SEN0401-I20-deck-ch08 (2 commits, 488146a, adc96d3). The checks and their results are those of the evidence nodes and the note of AC7_Deck_Ch08. Every check ran on the evidence bridge one after the other, on a machine with no other page test running. RENDERED REVIEW by the builder session (independent of the executor): the deck was rendered with soffice to PDF and PNG (51 pages, 800 x 450) and EVERY slide was viewed on its own, one image per slide, not in contact sheets. Seen: title, route, questions, concept map, seven dividers each with three or four concepts, a meaning line, an everyday comparison and the progress strip, concept-card slides with the everyday comparison under each meaning, twenty code slides each with the listing, a plain-language line under every step and the executed result as a table or a native chart, three story slides with 5N1K, picture, credit and live link, the policy/consensus slide, the BIP status chart, summary, resources and closing. Nothing overlaps, no text leaves its box, every slide is numbered. FINDINGS KEPT (none is an element of the criterion; the coordinator decides): (a) STORY PICTURES: the Schnorr slide (22) carries a real photograph of Claus-Peter Schnorr (Konrad Jacobs, Wikimedia Commons, credited); the signature-in-the-book slide (33) shows the Bitcoin logo and the Android slide (39) the Android robot, both real public pictures, credited and marked on the slide as stand-in pictures (no picture of the sender or of the incident was used); the deck standard check counts a real picture and passes D5, but the owner's story rule asks for real pictures of the story, so an event picture (for example a block explorer view of block 277,316 and the Bitcoin.org alert page) would serve it better. (b) The BIP status slide (48) is a native chart with counts taken from the saved BIP index of deck 1.1.0 and says so on the slide; it is not one of the twenty code examples, so its numbers are recorded, not run by this deck. (c) The WHO and WHERE chips of the three story slides are cut with an ellipsis (slides 22, 33, 39). (d) The route slide (2) lists eight entries under the words seven parts, and slide 4 counts seven parts. (e) Slides 8, 35 and several concept-card slides leave much empty space. (f) The closing slide lists four lines while slide 3 promises a return to seven questions. " .
