ex:TK_Ch01_Deck_Build_R24 a backlog:ExecutionTask ; rdfs:label "Rebuild the deck of chapter 1 to the deck standard with its chapter-code manifest"@en ; backlog:hasIdentifier "TK_Ch01_Deck_Build_R24" ; backlog:hasTitle "Rebuild the deck of chapter 1 to the deck standard with its chapter-code manifest" ;
    backlog:belongsToLineage ex:Lineage3 ; backlog:memberOfContainer ex:Iter_24 ; backlog:admittedByOutput ex:Out3_BacklogR28 ; backlog:responsible ex:Executor_ClaudeCodeSession ;
    backlog:hasInvestmentCategory backlog:Cat_NewCapability ; backlog:hasTaskType backlog:TaskType_Build ; backlog:effectiveDefinitionOfDone ex:DoD ; backlog:hasState backlog:Done ; backlog:startedAt "2026-10-25T09:59:10"^^xsd:dateTime ; backlog:finishedAt "2026-10-25T10:17:37"^^xsd:dateTime ; backlog:lastAuditedAt "2026-10-25T10:20:41"^^xsd:dateTime ; backlog:hasExecutionModality backlog:Mode_Hybrid ;
    backlog:notYetScoreable true ; backlog:hasScoreabilityReason "Ranked by its story's state; scoring both would double-count." ;
    backlog:hasAuditNote "Rebuild the deck of chapter 1 as a NEW file (the newest deck is 03-materials/ch01/SEN0401_Ch01_Introduction_3e_v2_11_0.pptx; it stays unchanged) with a new version number above the newest, from the chapter research 03-materials/ch01/rdodi and the earlier deck's assets, to the deck standard. MEASURED today (baseline, 2026-10-25): the deck fails D1, D2, D3, D4, D5, D7, D10 and D11 of the deck standard check v1_1_0 and the overlap check reports overlaps; there is no chapter-code manifest. The owner's words on the chapter 5 and 6 decks (quoted in ex:CR3_Remedy56) apply: a deck must be presentable and understandable. DECK STANDARD (checked by the deck standard check, python-pptx 1.0.2, one named result per element): D1 every slide is numbered; D2 no text box over 30 words; D3 every code slide has a plain-language explanation beside the code and the executed result as a visual (a native chart, a table or a drawn diagram), not REPL text; D4 every key concept of the chapter has an everyday analogy slide or box (the key concepts are listed in the chapter research); D5 every story has who, what, when, where, why and how, a real picture and a live link; D6 every chart is a native chart object, no chart picture; D7 speaker notes at most 60 words per slide; D8 a resources slide with links; D9 one background scheme, no dark/light switching mid-deck; D10 every divider names its concepts with one line of meaning and one visual; D11 coverage: the share of the chapter research concepts that appear on a slide, stated as measured, not below 90 percent; D12 no code line wraps. Rules from the owner's standing teaching-material preferences: numbered slides, short sentences, explanation beside every code listing, the executed result shown as a visualization (native chart, table or drawn diagram) next to each listing, an everyday analogy for each key concept, 5N1K stories with real names, a real picture and a live link, native charts only, a resources slide with links, short speaker notes, one smooth colour scheme (the matching look of the page). Follow the way the decks of chapters 5 to 8 were built (their generator files in 08-tooling/ch05-deck to ch08-deck are the pattern; write your own generator as a NEW tool file). ALSO write the chapter-code manifest 03-materials/ch01/page/chapter_code_ch01_v1_0_0.json in the form of chapter_code_ch08_v1_0_0.json: the ordered list of the deck's code examples (order number = slide order, a short NAME, the code that runs in the page with the chapter library it needs, the expected result). Slide code is exactly one chapter-code page tab: the manifest equals the deck's code boxes. NO example may have a first output line that starts with an error name (TypeError, ValueError, KeyError, Traceback ...): an example that teaches an error first prints a plain line, then the error is caught and printed after it. Run `python3 -B 08-tooling/sen0401_deck_control_check_v1_1_0.py manifest:01`, the deck standard check v1_1_0 and the deck overlap check v1_0_0 on your deck and fix until they pass. The checker is the independent tools, not you: report every element as printed. DO NOT EDIT OR APPEND TO ANY FILE COMMITTED EARLIER, INCLUDING YOUR OWN LOG FILES: write each log once, after its run is complete, and commit it once (the control of the page 9.82.0 of chapter 5 found a log file of the executor appended in a later commit and named that element not met). A committed file is never edited: every output is a new file with a new version number. STOP and report if the task cannot be satisfied without editing a committed file, a gate, a test or a threshold beyond the named new files. Report every result as printed (PASS, FAIL, NOT_MEASURED, CANNOT_RUN); a skip is not a pass. The machine has 2 cores: browser tests run one at a time and no build runs beside them. Planned session SEN0401-I24-deck-ch01; estimated start S (T0 = the first executor start of the iteration), about 1.5 hours (an estimate, G120: nobody has witnessed a start; the real start is recorded at work begun)." .

