    backlog:hasAuditNote "Make the NEW file 08-tooling/sen0401_deck_arc_check_v1_2_0.py from v1_1_0 (which stays byte-identical, SHA-256 before and after): (1) the number of slides is read from the deck (or the plan) unless --slides is given, so a deck of any length is checked against the rest of the rule; (2) a closing part of the agenda (summary, resources, closing) may start at the summary slide that precedes the resources slide, not only at a section slide; every other rule and message is unchanged. PROOF to keep as logs: the unified diff; SHA-256 of v1_1_0 before and after; v1_2_0 on the decks of chapters 1, 4 and 8 (and 5, 6, 7 for comparison): the refusals of the 26-slide demand and of the closing-part agenda range are gone, and every refusal that remains is one the check's own rule gives (listed); on a fixture deck with a wrong agenda range (a range starting inside a section) v1_2_0 still refuses. DO NOT EDIT OR APPEND TO ANY FILE COMMITTED EARLIER, INCLUDING YOUR OWN LOG FILES: write each log once, after its run is complete, and commit it once (the control of the page 9.82.0 of chapter 5 found a log file of the executor appended in a later commit and named that element not met). A committed file is never edited: every output is a new file with a new version number. STOP and report if the task cannot be satisfied without editing a committed file, a gate, a test or a threshold beyond the named new files. Report every result as printed (PASS, FAIL, NOT_MEASURED, CANNOT_RUN); a skip is not a pass. The machine has 2 cores: browser tests run one at a time and no build runs beside them. Planned session SEN0401-I28-arccheck; estimated start S (S = the start of the iteration, planned and not yet held), about 1.5 hours (an estimate, G120: nobody has witnessed a start; the real start is recorded at work begun)." .

